  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1239 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nHeat continues to be the big talking point for the remainder of\nthis week as model agreement does not see the the upper-level high\npressure staying over the Central and Western Untied States laying\noff anytime soon. With this high-pressure in place, the Panhandles\nwill hold onto a more stagnate and dry upper-level pattern that\nwill aid in forcing temperatures to warm clear into the weekend.\nThursday and Friday in particular could be the peak of this heat\nwith many models seeing the upper-level high extend further south\nand directly over the Panhandles. As it stand most models see the\nvast majority of the Panhandles in the triple digits with some of\nour more terrain based locations, such as the Palo Duro Canyon and\nCanadian River Valley, nearing the 110 degree mark Thursday\nafternoon. Obviously such hot temperatures for both days brings\nincreasing confidence of some form of heat related products being\nneeded. However, what may not be as obvious is that low\ntemperatures are not fairing much better with present expectations\nseeing locations, such as Amarillo, have the potential to either\nbreak or tie their record warm low temperature clear through\nSunday. Expectations like these would also mean that there is very\nlittle relief occurring during overnight hours which may only\namplify the risk of seeing heat related illness as each day\npasses. In short, the closing days of this week may see an much\nhigher risk for heat related illness not just from very hot\nafternoon, but from the accumulating effect of seeing these hot\ntemperatures for multiple day on top of getting very little relief\nfrom the heat overnight.",
